      "summary": "Move the live setlist into a single Setlist owner (#1758)",
      "description": "* Stop setlist Skip from reading past the end after quitting\n\nWhen PauseMenuManager.Skip() is called on the last song of a setlist, it\ncalls Quit() but then falls through to the advance code. Quit() goes\nthrough GameManager.ForceQuitSong(), which resets GlobalVariables.State,\nso the fall-through increments ShowIndex and indexes the now-empty\nShowSongs list, throwing ArgumentOutOfRangeException.\n\nSetlistPause hides Skip on the last song, so this is a guard against a\npath that shouldn't normally be reachable, but the guard itself crashed.\nReturn after Quit().\n\nVerified by reading the call path; there are no tests for this code.\nBuilt the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with dotnet: no new\nerrors.\n\n* Move the live setlist into a single Setlist owner\n\nThe setlist lived in two places. While it was being built it was\nMusicLibraryMenu.ShowPlaylist, an instance field that disappeared with\nthe menu scene. Start Set copied it into GlobalVariables.State.ShowSongs,\nShowIndex and PlayingAShow, which the score screen, pause menu and\nDifficulty Select then read. Single-song plays from the library and\nfrom careers also overwrote ShowSongs with one song, only so that\nDifficulty Select's harmony loop had something to iterate. Nothing could\nobserve the setlist, or follow it from building into a show.\n\nAdd YARG.Playlists.Setlist, a static owner holding:\n\n- Playlist: the ephemeral playlist the music library edits, unchanged,\n  so the library's generic playlist code (move, sort, remove, delete)\n  keeps working on it.\n- IsPlaying and Index: the show's position, replacing PlayingAShow and\n  ShowIndex. Start(), TryAdvance() and EndShow() move it.\n- Revision and Changed: bumped and raised on every edit and every\n  position change. Nothing in-game subscribes yet; the setlist file\n  contract will build on them.\n\nPlaylist gains an Edited event, raised from the existing post-edit hook\n(renamed from SaveAfterManualEdit to OnEdited, since it now does more\nthan save) and from Clear() when it removes anything. Saving behavior\nfor non-ephemeral playlists is unchanged.\n\nThe show indexes into Setlist.Songs, which is Playlist.ToList(): the\nsame resolved list that Start Set used to snapshot into ShowSongs. It is\nnot an index into the raw hashes, because SongsByHash is filtered by the\ncontent-rating setting, so a hash can stop resolving without a rescan.\n\nLifetimes are kept as they were, with one exception noted below:\n\n- GlobalVariables.LoadScene() resets the setlist whenever the menu scene\n  loads. Every such call comes from gameplay, the score screen,\n  calibration, content or startup, which are exactly the cases where the\n  old MusicLibraryMenu instance was destroyed and recreated empty.\n  ExitLibrary and the delete actions still clear it explicitly.\n- Every place that set PlayingAShow = false now calls Setlist.EndShow(),\n  as do the History replay paths that reset GlobalVariables.State.\n  GameManager.ForceQuitSong() also resets State, but it then loads the\n  menu, so the menu-load reset covers it.\n- Difficulty Select's harmony loop iterates its own _songList, which\n  already holds either the show or the single song, so the ShowSongs\n  overwrite on single plays is gone. The results are the same: replay\n  paths used to see an empty ShowSongs and fall back to the song's own\n  part count, which is what a one-song list gives.\n\nThe exception: when a show song fails to load, gameplay returns to the\nmenu without resetting State, so PlayingAShow used to stay true in the\nmenu until something else cleared it. The menu-load reset now ends the\nshow there too.\n\nGameManager.ShowIndex was an unused serialized field; it's removed along\nwith its entry in Gameplay.unity.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet against this branch (0 errors), after confirming that the build\nreports a deliberately introduced error in Setlist.cs. Not yet run in\nthe Unity editor.\n\n* Refresh library navigation from Setlist.Changed\n\nThe library's green button adds to the setlist while it's empty and\nstarts the set once it has songs, so the navigation scheme (and the\nsidebar's play button label it updates) must be rebuilt when the setlist\ngoes from empty to non-empty or back. Three call sites did that by hand:\nafter adding the first song, after adding a playlist to an empty\nsetlist, and after deleting the setlist from the popup. Other edit\npaths, such as removing the last song from the popup, only came out\nright because they also called Refresh(), which rebuilds the scheme.\n\nMusicLibraryMenu now subscribes to Setlist.Changed while it's enabled.\nOn an empty/non-empty transition it rebuilds the scheme right away,\nor, if a popup or dialog is open, sets the existing\n_needsNavigationSchemeRefresh flag. For a popup, PopupMenu.OnDisable\nconsumes that flag synchronously when the popup closes; for a dialog,\nUpdate() picks it up on the first frame after the dialog closes. This is\nthe same pattern Refresh() uses. The three manual rebuilds are removed.\n\nThis also fixes, by reading the code (not yet confirmed in the editor),\n\"Add to Setlist\" from the popup on an empty setlist. The old code\nrebuilt the scheme while the popup was still open, which popped the\npopup's scheme instead of the library's; the popup's OnDisable then\npopped the new library scheme, leaving the old empty-setlist labels in\nplace. Adding a playlist from the popup did the same, but recovered\nbecause its Refresh() had also set the flag.\n\nRebuilds still happen in Show mode's paths exactly as before:\nSetNavigationScheme() returns early while MenuState is Show, and\nLeaveShowMode() rebuilds on exit. Two paths now rebuild once more than\nbefore, harmlessly: deleting the setlist from the playlist view (once on\nClear(), once in ExitPlaylistView()), and leaving the library with a\nnon-empty setlist (ExitLibrary's Clear() rebuilds just before PopMenu()\npops the scheme). The pushes and pops stay balanced and nothing renders\nin between.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* Follow hash changes in the setlist after a rescan\n\nA rescan maps songs whose chart changed at the same location to their\nnew hash, and PlaylistContainer.ReplaceUpdatedSongHashes() rewrites\nfavorites and saved playlists to match. The setlist isn't one of those\nplaylists, so its old hash stopped resolving and the song silently\ndropped out of the setlist, even though it was still in the library.\n\nExtract the per-playlist replacement loop into\nPlaylistContainer.ReplaceHashes() and add\nSetlist.ReplaceUpdatedSongHashes(), which applies the same replacements\nto the setlist without saving and raises Changed if anything moved.\nSongContainer.RunRefresh() computes the replacements once and passes\nthem to both. The setlist call runs after FillContainers(), so a Changed\nsubscriber that resolves Setlist.Songs sees the new library.\n\nHashes that no longer resolve are left alone, as before: SongsByHash is\nalso filtered by the content-rating setting, so dropping them would lose\nsongs that are only hidden.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* End the show whenever Difficulty Select is popped\n\nStart Set marks the setlist as playing before pushing Difficulty Select.\nIf the player then backs out, the setlist stayed marked as playing while\nthey were back in the library. The ways out are Back on the first\nplayer, every player sitting out, only bots ahead in a career, and the\nheader's back arrow, which DifficultySelectMenu.prefab wires to\nMenuObject.CloseMenu() and so pops the menu without going through\nDifficultySelectMenu's code at all.\n\nNothing visible depends on that today: every other route into gameplay\n(a single song from the library, a career song, History replays) ends\nthe show first, and Start Set restarts it. But Setlist.IsPlaying, Index\nand Changed are what the setlist state file will publish, and it would\nreport a show in progress that never started.\n\nDifficultySelectMenu.OnDisable() now ends the show when the menu is no\nlonger on MenuManager's stack, which covers every route that pops it.\nLoading gameplay and opening a menu on top also disable it, but leave it\non the stack (or, during scene unload, may find MenuManager already\ndestroyed), so they don't end the show. MenuManager gains a small\nIsOpen(menu) query for this.\n\nExitLibrary now calls Setlist.Reset() instead of clearing the playlist,\nso leaving the library can't leave a show marked as playing either.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* Make the Setlist API safe to call when nothing resolves\n\nReview of the Setlist owner found several weak spots:\n\n- Start() set IsPlaying, bumped Revision and raised Changed before\n  indexing the first song, and its only caller checked the raw hash\n  count rather than the resolved songs. A setlist whose only songs had\n  been deleted from disk (Count 1, no resolvable songs) threw\n  ArgumentOutOfRangeException after announcing a show that never\n  started. The old code threw too (ShowSongs.First()), after setting\n  PlayingAShow. It is now TryStart(out first): it resolves the songs once\n  and returns false, changing nothing, when none resolve. StartSetlist\n  branches on it, so Start Set with nothing playable does nothing\n  instead of throwing.\n- TryAdvance() resolved the whole song list before checking IsPlaying,\n  so every score-screen Continue outside a show paid for it. It checks\n  IsPlaying first now.\n- Songs was a property that allocated and resolved on every read. It is\n  now ResolveSongs(), so the cost shows at the call site.\n- Changed was invoked bare. It fires inside GlobalVariables.LoadScene()\n  (via Reset()) after menu input is disabled, so a throwing subscriber,\n  such as the planned state-file writer hitting an IO error, would abort\n  the scene load and leave input disabled. Each handler now runs in its\n  own try/catch and failures are logged.\n\nDocs corrected: ResolveSongs() names the content-rating settings rather\nthan \"a filter\" (the library's Filters menu doesn't affect it), and\nnotes that a sort drops unresolved hashes. Changed documents that it\nfires before callers of TryStart/TryAdvance update CurrentSong.\nPlaylist.Edited says it can fire on calls that change nothing.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* Drop duplicate setlist songs created by a hash remap\n\nFindUpdatedSongHashes() maps a vanished hash A to the hash C of the\nchart now at A's location, even when C is already elsewhere in the\nsetlist. Remapping [A, C] then gives [C, C]: a song AddSong() would never\nlet in twice. The show would play it twice, and RemoveSong() would only\nremove the first copy.\n\nAfter remapping, Setlist.ReplaceUpdatedSongHashes() keeps the first\noccurrence of each hash. It only happens when two charts converge on one\nhash, so it's rare. Saved playlists have the same gap in\nPlaylistContainer.ReplaceUpdatedSongHashes(); that predates this branch\nand is left alone.\n\nRemoving entries can't shift Setlist.Index under a playing song: rescans\nonly run in the menu, and the only time one can run while a show is\nmarked as playing is from Difficulty Select (through the rescan toast),\nwhere Index is still 0 and the first occurrence is always kept.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* Return to the Playlists list when the saved setlist view is empty\n\nMusicLibraryMenu remembers the playlist it was showing in the static\n_savedPlaylist and reopens on it after a partial reload, such as\nreturning from gameplay. Starting a set from Playlists > Current Setlist\nsaves the setlist itself there.\n\nBefore this branch, that saved object was the old library instance's own\nShowPlaylist, kept alive only by the static reference after the menu\nscene unloaded. Returning from the show reopened on that leftover copy:\nit listed the songs just played, but Start Set read the new instance's\nempty setlist and did nothing, Delete and the move buttons edited the\ncopy, and Current Setlist was missing from the Playlists list. With the\nsingle Setlist owner, the saved object is the real setlist, which the\nmenu-load reset has emptied, so the view showed only its buttons.\n\nWhen the view being restored is the setlist and it is empty, reopen on\nthe Playlists list instead, the way entering it from the library does:\nclear the preview, rebuild the navigation scheme for PlaylistSelect, and\nselect the top of the list. This is a deliberate, visible change from\nthe leftover-copy behavior. Once the setlist survives a show (PR 1b),\nthe saved view will have songs again and reopen as before.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors). Not yet run in the Unity editor.\n\n* End the show before ChangePlayer pops Difficulty Select\n\nDifficultySelectMenu.OnEnable() calls ChangePlayer(0), which can pop the\nmenu straight away: when only bots precede the first player in a career\nrun, or when every player ends up sitting out. MenuObject and\nDifficultySelectMenu share a GameObject, and Unity won't deactivate an\nobject while it's still being activated (\"GameObject is already being\nactivated or deactivated\"), so in that case OnDisable() never runs and\nthe show stays marked as playing.\n\nIt's reachable from a library set: GlobalVariables.State.CurrentCareer\nis set when a career song is started and nothing clears it if the\nplayer backs out, so a later Start Set with only bot players takes the\ncareer branch. That leak predates this branch and is left for a\nseparate fix.\n\nCall Setlist.EndShow() directly before the two PopMenu() calls in\nChangePlayer. Outside OnEnable, OnDisable() then finds the show already\nended and does nothing.\n\nBased on code reading and that Unity error; not reproduced in the\neditor. Verified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp\nproject with dotnet (0 errors).\n\n* Clarify setlist fallback, log remap duplicates, note silent Start Set\n\nThree small follow-ups from review:\n\n- The comment on the empty-setlist fallback in MusicLibraryMenu.OnEnable\n  named only the menu-load reset as its trigger. A reused library in the\n  same scene can also restore a setlist view that was emptied while it\n  was disabled, and falling back is right there too, so the comment now\n  describes the condition rather than one cause.\n- Setlist.ReplaceUpdatedSongHashes() logs how many duplicates it\n  removed, not only how many hashes it replaced.\n- StartSetlist gets a TODO: when none of the setlist's songs resolve,\n  TryStart() fails and Start Set does nothing, without telling the\n  player. It used to throw, so this is no worse, but it should say why.\n\nVerified by building the Unity-generated Assembly-CSharp project with\ndotnet (0 errors).\n\n* End any show from MusicLibraryMenu.OnEnable instead of at each exit\n\nStart Set marks the show as playing before pushing Difficulty Select, so\nbacking out has to end it again. That was guarded in seven places: an\nOnDisable check in DifficultySelectMenu that the menu had been popped\n(using a new MenuManager.IsOpen query), two EndShow() calls in\nChangePlayer for pops that run inside OnEnable, where Unity won't\ndeactivate the menu, and EndShow() before the library, career and both\nHistory entry points. Each new route in or out had to remember one.\n\nEvery one of those cases reduces to a single fact. Start Set can only be\nreached from the music library (its button, green hold and popup item\nall live there), and every way out of Difficulty Select short of\ngameplay pops back to the library: Back, the header's back arrow,\neveryone sitting out, or only bots ahead in a career. The library is a\nseparate GameObject, so PopMenu() re-activates it even when Difficulty\nSelect is stuck mid-activation. So the library ends any show in its own\nOnEnable, and no show can be marked as playing while the library, or\nanything reached from it after leaving, is on screen.\n\nRemove the seven guards and MenuManager.IsOpen. ExitLibrary goes back to\nclearing the playlist, as upstream does. A comment on the new call notes\nthat anything else that starts a show will need its own way to end it.\n\nVerified by building the Assembly-CSharp project that the open Unity\neditor generated for this worktree, with dotnet (0 errors). Not yet\nre-run in the editor."
    }
